<% content_for :title, "DBT & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-copy,
  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-minds > div,
  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-dialectic > div,
  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-practice-list > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-copy ul {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.3em;
    list-style: disc;
  }

  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-copy li + li {
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
  }

  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-skill-title {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.125rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .dbt-bipolar-page .dbt-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>

<main class="dbt-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="dbt-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="dbt-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="dbt-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / DBT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>DBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="dbt-bipolar-intro">
        ACCEPT WHAT IS.<br>
        CHANGE WHAT YOU CAN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / DIALECTICS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink" aria-labelledby="dbt-dialectics-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        01 / DIALECTICS
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-dialectics-title">
        TWO THINGS<br>
        CAN BE<br>
        TRUE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>Changing reality requires first accepting reality.</p>
        <cite class="dbt-source">— Marsha M. Linehan</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        DBT is about teaching you how to experience intense emotions without automatically
        reacting to them, tolerate distress without making your circumstances worse, and
        develop healthier relationships with yourself and others. It is about accepting
        yourself as you are while recognizing that some of your behaviors need to change.
        You have valid emotions, but they don’t have to control your actions. DBT places
        equal importance on acceptance and change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dialectical thinking is the foundation of DBT. It is the understanding that two
        seemingly contradictory things can be true at the same time. For example, you can
        accept yourself the way you are but at the same time acknowledge that you need to
        change certain behaviors. You can recognize that bipolar disorder influences your
        actions while still taking responsibility for their consequences. This approach
        challenges all-or-nothing thinking. Acceptance doesn't mean that nothing needs to
        change. Change doesn't mean that who you are right now is unacceptable.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-dialectic">
        <div><span>THIS IS HARD.</span></div>
        <b>+</b>
        <div><span>I CAN HANDLE THE NEXT PART.</span></div>
        <b>=</b>
        <div><span>BOTH CAN BE TRUE.</span></div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / WISE MIND -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-yellow" aria-labelledby="dbt-wise-mind-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        02 / WISE MIND
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-wise-mind-title">
        FEELINGS.<br>
        FACTS.<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        There are three “minds” in DBT:
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-minds">
        <div>
          <span>EMOTION MIND</span>
      <p>
        Feelings strongly influence thinking and behavior
      </p>
      <p>
        May act impulsively, interpret situations through intense emotions, make decisions
        based on what feels necessary in the moment
      </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>REASONABLE (LOGICAL) MIND</span>
      <p>
        Emphasizes facts, logic, objective information
      </p>
      <p>
        Focus on practical consequences while overlooking emotional needs
      </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WISE MIND</span>
      <p>
        Integrates emotional and logical
      </p>
      <p>
        Allows you to acknowledge what you are feeling while considering reality, your
        values, and the potential consequences of your actions
      </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        For you, with bipolar disorder, Wise Mind may also mean relying on an established
        safety plan, professional guidance, and trusted outside perspectives. You don’t have
        to ignore your emotions to make reasonable decisions. You can listen to them without
        allowing them to make every decision for you.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MINDFULNESS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-black" aria-labelledby="dbt-mindfulness-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        03 / MINDFULNESS
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-mindfulness-title">
        NOTICE<br>
        BEFORE YOU<br>
        REACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Mindfulness is a core skill in DBT. While experiencing symptoms, DBT encourages you
        to recognize the experiences without automatically identifying with them. You don’t
        say you are angry; instead say you are experiencing anger. Create a distinction
        between the experiences and your identity. DBT emphasizes three mindfulness skills:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Observe: Notice your emotions, thoughts, and sensations without immediately
          reacting.
        </li>
        <li>
          Describe: Put your experience into words without adding judgments or
          assumptions.
        </li>
        <li>
          Participate: Engage fully in what you're doing rather than becoming trapped in
          your thoughts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Mindfulness doesn’t require a perfectly calm mind, just an observant one. You are
        simply experiencing things, not becoming them.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-purple" aria-labelledby="dbt-distress-tolerance-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        04 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-distress-tolerance-title">
        SURVIVE<br>
        THE FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Distress tolerance is another key ability: experiencing emotional pain without
        trying to escape, eliminate, or act on it.
      </p>

      <p>
        With bipolar disorder, difficult emotions can feel unbearable. Instinct says to do
        something about it to provide immediate relief like drinking or another impulsive
        decision, even if that “solution” causes problems. DBT teaches you that not every
        painful situation can be resolved immediately and that your goal should be to get
        through it safely. Use the STOP skill:
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-practice-list">
        <div>
          <span>S</span>
      <p>
        Stop: Resist the urge to react immediately.
      </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>T</span>
      <p>
        Take a step back: Create distance from the situation.
      </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>O</span>
      <p>
        Observe: Notice what you're feeling, thinking, and wanting to do.
      </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>P</span>
      <p>
        Proceed mindfully: Choose an action based on your goals rather than your immediate
        impulse.
      </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Other distress tolerance strategies are paced breathing, grounding exercises,
        temporary distractions, self-soothing, and considering the consequences of acting on
        an urge.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you want to drink, talk to someone right away about the urge and remove yourself
        from the triggering environment. If you cannot remove yourself—like if you're having
        a manic episode—talk to the psychiatrist and talk to someone you can trust, report
        your mental state and ask for help. Don’t pretend you’re fine and don’t need help;
        don’t convince yourself your emotions are unreasonable.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don’t have to feel better immediately, and sometimes the most important
        accomplishment is getting through a difficult moment without making the situation
        worse.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / EMOTION REGULATION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink" aria-labelledby="dbt-emotion-regulation-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        05 / EMOTION REGULATION
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-emotion-regulation-title">
        NAME THE<br>
        DAMN THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Emotional regulation is important. It involves recognizing, understanding, and
        responding to your emotions in ways that support your well-being by identifying them
        rather than categorizing them as simply good or bad. With bullshit bipolar, there’s
        an extra layer of changes in mood, energy, sleep, and cognition all influencing your
        emotions. You can ask yourself:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          What emotion am I experiencing?
        </li>
        <li>
          What happened before I started feeling this way?
        </li>
        <li>
          What thoughts are influencing this emotion?
        </li>
        <li>
          What is this emotion urging me to do?
        </li>
        <li>
          Does the intensity of my emotion fit the current circumstances?
        </li>
        <li>
          What response would help me in the long term?
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / OPPOSITE ACTION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-yellow" aria-labelledby="dbt-opposite-action-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        06 / OPPOSITE ACTION
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-opposite-action-title">
        DON'T FEED<br>
        EVERY<br>
        FEELING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        You need to check the facts, asking yourself what evidence you have for your
        emotions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Another technique is Opposite Action, which involves choosing a behavior that
        counters an unhelpful emotional urge when that emotion or its intensity doesn’t fit
        the facts. If shame urges you to isolate, reach out instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can’t always choose how you feel, but you can practice choosing what you do with
        those feelings.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / INTERPERSONAL EFFECTIVENESS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-purple" aria-labelledby="dbt-relationships-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        07 / RELATIONSHIPS
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-relationships-title">
        SAY WHAT<br>
        YOU FUCKING<br>
        MEAN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Bipolar disorder, as you know, can really mess up your shit—especially the ways you
        communicate, respond to conflict, and maintain relationships. During periods of
        emotional intensity, you might become defensive, withdraw from others, react
        angrily, or struggle to express your needs. You also can experience frustration when
        people misunderstand your diagnosis or interpret your symptoms as character flaws.
      </p>

      <p>
        DBT teaches interpersonal effectiveness: the ability to communicate your needs,
        establish boundaries, maintain relationships, and preserve your self-respect. Three
        important DBT approaches are:
      </p>

      <h3 class="dbt-skill-title">DEAR MAN</h3>

      <p>
        Helps you communicate requests, express your needs, and negotiate effectively:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          D — Describe: State the facts without judgment.
        </li>
        <li>
          E — Express: Explain your feelings and opinions.
        </li>
        <li>
          A — Assert: Clearly ask for what you need or say no.
        </li>
        <li>
          R — Reinforce: Explain the benefits of your request.
        </li>
        <li>
          M — Mindful: Stay focused on your goal.
        </li>
        <li>
          A — Appear confident: Use a steady voice and confident posture.
        </li>
        <li>
          N — Negotiate: Be willing to discuss alternatives.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="dbt-skill-title">GIVE</h3>

      <p>
        Helps you maintain relationships through gentleness, interest, validation, and an
        easy manner:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          G — Gentle: Avoid attacks, threats, and judgment.
        </li>
        <li>
          I — Interested: Listen and show interest in the other person.
        </li>
        <li>
          V — Validate: Acknowledge their feelings and perspective.
        </li>
        <li>
          E — Easy manner: Be warm and approachable; use humor when appropriate.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="dbt-skill-title">FAST</h3>

      <p>
        Helps you maintain self-respect through fairness, appropriate apologies, honesty,
        and staying true to your values:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          F — Fair: Be fair to yourself and others.
        </li>
        <li>
          A — No unnecessary Apologies: Don’t apologize for having needs, feelings, or
          boundaries.
        </li>
        <li>
          S — Stick to your values: Stay true to what matters to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          T — Truthful: Be honest; avoid exaggerating or making excuses.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These skills encourage you to balance three important goals: obtaining what you
        need, maintaining healthy relationships, and respecting yourself. It’s not required
        to abandon your needs for a relationship, excuse your behavior, or tolerate
        mistreatment. Healthy relationships require communication, accountability,
        boundaries, and mutual respect.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / RADICAL ACCEPTANCE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink" aria-labelledby="dbt-radical-acceptance-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        08 / RADICAL ACCEPTANCE
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-radical-acceptance-title">
        REALITY<br>
        DOESN'T NEED<br>
        PERMISSION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Radical acceptance: an old friend. You did this when you went to jail, when you
        accepted that you have bipolar disorder and ADHD, when you accepted your trauma.
        Radical acceptance is acknowledging reality as it is rather than continually
        fighting against something that happened or is happening. You don’t have to like it,
        but you just accept it and deal with it best you can—which you do.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-black" aria-labelledby="dbt-practice-title">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        09 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2 id="dbt-practice-title">
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        During episodes, try to respond rather than react. Manic episodes are a huge pain,
        and DBT encourages you to notice behavioral changes and create safeguards before
        your judgment becomes significantly impaired. Get support when critically needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        In a depressive episode, DBT teaches you to acknowledge your suffering without
        adding unnecessary self-judgment. Don’t criticize yourself for struggling; practice
        self-compassion and break responsibilities into manageable steps. Your progress
        doesn’t have to be dramatic to matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        During a mixed episode, the symptom combination can make the emotional reactions
        hard to manage; DBT encourages you to slow down responses, avoid impulse decisions,
        and use crisis-survival skills. Recognize your warning signs and seek timely
        treatment. This means to use your impulsivity plan and lean on others and your
        treatment team for help.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 / IMPULSIVITY & ADDICTION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-yellow" aria-labelledby="dbt-impulsivity-title">
    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">10 / IMPULSIVITY &amp; ADDICTION</p>
      <h2 id="dbt-impulsivity-title">
        AN URGE<br>IS NOT AN<br>OBLIGATION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Impulsivity and addiction are two areas where you struggle the most. You get the
        “fuck its”. It comes about with mania mostly. DBT teaches you to recognize that an
        urge is an experience, not an obligation. You can notice a desire to act without
        immediately following it. Examine the urge, turn it over in your hands. Study it.
        This will get easier through growth, practice, planning, and support. Hone your
        impulsivity plan and stick to it.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 11 / SELF-VALIDATION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-purple" aria-labelledby="dbt-self-validation-title">
    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">11 / SELF-VALIDATION</p>
      <h2 id="dbt-self-validation-title">
        JUDGE THE<br>ACTION.<br>NOT YOUR WORTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        You used to feel a lot of shame about having bipolar disorder, and that may return
        again someday. You are embarrassed by it sometimes still. You still disappoint
        yourself. You regret decisions you make during episodes and get frustrated by the
        bullshit your symptoms have pulled. You also can internalize the stigma.
      </p>

      <p>
        You need to try practicing self-validation which means acknowledging that your
        emotions and experiences make sense within the context of your circumstances. It
        doesn’t mean your conclusions are accurate or your actions are justified though. DBT
        teaches you to separate judgment from observation. Make judgments about the actions
        or situation, not who you are as a person.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 12 / A LIFE WORTH LIVING -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink" aria-labelledby="dbt-life-worth-living-title">
    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">12 / A LIFE WORTH LIVING</p>
      <h2 id="dbt-life-worth-living-title">
        BUILD A LIFE<br>BEYOND THE<br>CRISIS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        A goal central to DBT is helping you build a life worth living. This means
        developing a life that reflects your values, relationships, interests, and
        aspirations rather than organizing your entire existence around emotional crises.
      </p>

      <p>
        You spend a boatload of time managing things and recovering, worrying, and stressing
        all because of this bogus illness. DBT encourages you to look ahead and build
        positive experiences, develop a sense of competence, and work toward meaningful
        goals. Every symptom won’t disappear, so participate in your own life. You can build
        a life that isn’t bipolar-centric.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="dbt-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="dbt-ending-small">
        YOU CAN ACCEPT THIS MOMENT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        AND STILL<br>
        <span>CHANGE IT.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="dbt-ending-copy">

      <p>
        DBT says two things can be true at once. And it is true: you are doing the best you
        can with the skills, resources, and circumstances available to you, and you can
        continue learning skills that help you do better.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don’t have to choose between acceptance and change nor between acknowledging
        your suffering and taking responsibility for your life.
      </p>

      <p>
        You are not your diagnosis.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "dbt-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>
